- Mobile-first always. Default Tailwind styles = mobile (390px). md: and above = desktop. Bottom tab bar on mobile, sidebar on desktop. No exceptions at any phase.
- XP never written directly. All XP changes go through a single addXP(amount, eventType, description) function. It writes to xp_log first, then profile XP recalculates from the sum. The log is always the source of truth.
- quest_log is append-only. Never update or delete rows. It's the permanent record.
- Capacity is derived, not stored. capacity_slots_used = count of clients where status = 'active'. Always calculate live.
- Streak runs on page load. Check if yesterday has a completed daily in quest_log. If not, reset streak. No cron needed yet.
- Rank check runs after every XP event. If new total crosses threshold AND milestone conditions pass, trigger rank-up.
Quest assignment is manual in MVP. You press "Generate Today's Quests." Auto-midnight assignment is deferred.

PROJECT ARCHITECTURE RULES
File Structure
src/
├── components/        # Reusable UI components only
│   ├── ui/            # Primitives — Button, Card, Badge, Input etc.
│   └── [feature]/     # Feature-specific components — QuestCard, ClientRow etc.
├── pages/             # One file per route/page. Thin — layout + composition only.
├── hooks/             # Custom hooks. One concern per file.
├── services/          # All Supabase calls live here. Nothing else.
├── utils/             # Pure functions. No side effects, no API calls.
├── constants/         # XP values, rank thresholds, config. No magic numbers in code.
├── types/             # TypeScript types/interfaces if you go TS, or JSDoc shapes
└── context/           # Global state — auth, profile. Keep this small.

The Rules
1. No monolith files.
A file does one thing. A component renders one concern. A hook manages one piece of state or one data flow. A service file handles one Supabase table or one domain. If you have to scroll more than a screen to understand what a file does, it's already too big.
2. Max 150 lines per file — soft.
The exception condition you described exactly. 120 lines that are coherent and tightly related = fine. 90 lines doing three unrelated things = split it now. The question isn't length, it's coherence. Ask: "does everything in this file serve the same single purpose?" If no, split.
3. Pages are dumb.
Pages do not contain logic, API calls, or complex state. They import components, arrange layout, and pass data down. If a page file is growing past 80 lines something is wrong — extract it.
4. Components don't fetch.
Components receive data via props or read from a hook. They do not call Supabase directly. Ever. All data fetching lives in hooks (using React Query) which call services. The chain is always: page/component → hook → service → Supabase.
5. Services are just functions.
A service file is a collection of plain async functions. No state, no hooks, no React. Just: take input, talk to Supabase, return data or throw. Example — clientService.js contains getClients(), addClient(), updateClientStatus(). Nothing else.
6. Hooks own the data layer.
Every React Query useQuery or useMutation lives in a custom hook. useClients(), useProfile(), useQuests(). The component calls the hook and gets back data + loading + error. Clean.
7. No magic numbers.
XP values, rank thresholds, quest limits, streak rules — all live in constants/. If you write: xp += 200 inside a function, reject it. It should be xp += XP_EVENTS.CLIENT_SIGNED orwhatever the variable name is. 
8. One component per file.
No exporting two components from the same file. The filename matches the component name exactly. QuestCard.jsx exports QuestCard. That's it.
9. Prop drilling stops at 2 levels.
If a prop is being passed through more than two components to get where it's needed, it goes into context or a shared hook instead. Don't let data tunnel through the tree.
10. Mutations always invalidate.
Every React Query mutation that changes data must invalidate the relevant query cache after success. If you add a client and the client list doesn't refresh, the cache invalidation is missing. Non-negotiable.
11. Error and loading states are not optional.
Every data-dependent component handles three states: loading, error, and success. No exceptions. Even if loading is just a skeleton and error is just a text fallback.
12. No inline styles.
Tailwind classes only. If a style can't be expressed in Tailwind, it goes in a CSS module file for that component only — not a global stylesheet.
13. Supabase client is a singleton.
One file — src/services/supabase.js — initialises and exports the Supabase client. Nothing else imports createClient. Everything imports from that one file.
14. Business logic lives in utils.
Streak calculation, XP total derivation, rank resolution from XP — these are pure functions in utils/. They take inputs, return outputs, touch nothing external. This makes them testable and reusable without pulling in any React.
15. addXP is the only door.
As defined in the build rules above — no direct writes to profile.xp anywhere. The addXP util/service function is the only entry point. You must never shortcut this.